{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01Srux3KoVcgyZ77bEU2fh9n","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. I wrote **`ai-engineering/answers/04-001-when-fine-tune-vs-prompt-engineering.md`** — the answer note for Q1: *When fine-tune vs prompt engineering?* ⭐\n\n**What's in the note:**\n\n- **Framing:** Probes production maturity — will you jump to fine-tuning prematurely, or work the cost ladder?\n- **Answer:** Three-gap framework (knowledge / behavior / efficiency), then a 5-rung decision ladder: prompt → RAG → SFT+LoRA → DPO → full fine-tune, with concrete thresholds for each step (data size, latency signals, iteration cost calculus)\n- **Example:** Code-review assistant — LoRA on 5K labeled diffs (Llama 3 8B, rank=32) → 95% cost reduction vs GPT-4o, ROUGE-L 0.71→0.84, with a comparison table across all 5 approaches\n- **Verbal script:** First-person, interview-voiced walkthrough with the \"type of gap\" framing as the opener\n- **Pitfalls:** Jumping straight to fine-tune, claiming it injects facts (it changes behavior), skipping catastrophic forgetting\n- **Cross-links:** Q2 (LoRA mechanics), Q5 (fine-tune vs RAG), Q40 (cross-category LLM fundamentals framing)\n\nNext up: `04-002-what-is-peftlora-and-when-use-it.md` — Q2 on PEFT/LoRA mechanics."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":340,"cache_read_input_tokens":67622,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":340,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"033af5f0-2340-40cf-b842-7cbf193e18cb","uuid":"325f3f56-f408-4bf6-93af-a129a6778788"}
